  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic map of CERN’s accelerator complex, showing how beams move through a chain of machines toward major experiments. The largest ring is the LHC, linked to the main detectors ALICE, ATLAS, CMS, and LHCb. Inside it sits the SPS, and below are the older injector stages such as the PS Booster, PS, LEIR, and LINACs, illustrating a layered hierarchy in which particles are produced, pre-accelerated, and then transferred upward into larger rings.\n\nColored beamlines connect the machines to fixed-target and specialized facilities, indicating several particle types and destinations. Protons and ions appear to originate in LINAC 4 or LINAC 3, pass through the Booster and PS, then continue to the SPS and finally the LHC. Other branches feed secondary-beam facilities such as ISOLDE, n_TOF, the East and North Areas, and experiments like AWAKE, HiRadMat, AD, ELENA, and CLEAR. Antiproton production and deceleration are also represented around AD and ELENA.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes CERN as an interconnected network rather than a single collider: multiple accelerators of different sizes and commissioning dates work together to deliver beams for high-energy collisions, antimatter studies, neutron experiments, and test-beam research.",
